    {
      "id": "canon-standard-section-7",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "6. A window of its own shape",
      "position": {
        "x": -630,
        "y": 220
      },
      "width": 620,
      "height": 380,
      "data": {
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "title": "6. A window of its own shape",
        "body": "The graph's window (the root's membrane bounds) is authored in the Detail's shape and holds everything the graph has.\n\n*Why:* A new graph is the size of a graph, and its Detail fills it without stretching.\n\n*Checked by:* Test (mechanical).",
